<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter11_designing-recoverable-programs">
    <title>Designing Recoverable Programs</title>

    <p>
        Exception handling is most useful when it is part of a clear division
        of responsibility. Lower-level code detects and describes a failure.
        Higher-level code decides whether it can recover, retry, report the
        problem, or stop the operation.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_layered-error-handling">
        <title>Handle a Failure at the Right Level</title>

        <p>
            A function should not catch an exception merely because it can. It
            should catch the exception when it knows what meaningful action to
            take. Otherwise, it should allow the exception to propagate to a
            caller with more context.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter11_recovery-responsibilities">
            <title>Dividing error-handling responsibilities</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="72%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Program layer</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Responsibility</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Low-level operation</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Detect the failure and throw a meaningful exception.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Intermediate operation</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Add context, recover, or allow propagation to continue.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Top-level interaction</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Give the user a clear message and choose the next action.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_user-facing-recovery">
        <title>Give Users a Useful Recovery Path</title>

        <p>
            A user-facing handler should explain what failed in plain language
            and, when possible, tell the user what to do next. A diagnostic
            message for a programmer can be more detailed, but it should not
            expose unnecessary implementation details to a user.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_recoverable-score">
            <title>Handling invalid input at the interaction boundary</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_recoverable-score"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

void process_score(int score) {
    if (score < 0 || score > 100) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("score must be between 0 and 100");
    }

    std::cout << "Accepted score: " << score << '\n';
}

int main() {
    try {
        process_score(120);
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& error) {
        std::cout << "Please try again: " << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The low-level function identifies the invalid score. The top-level
            code chooses a user-facing response. In a larger program, the
            interaction could ask for a new score instead of ending.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_recovery-guidelines">
	   <title>Guidelines for Recoverable Programs</title>

	<p>Follow these guidelines for Recoverable Programs:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Throw an exception that describes the failure.</li>
  <li>Catch an exception only where meaningful action is possible.</li>
  <li>Preserve the object's invariants after a failed operation.</li>
  <li>Use RAII so cleanup remains reliable during propagation.</li>
  <li>Keep user messages clear and avoid silently ignoring failures.</li>
</ol>
        <p>
            These guidelines prepare the program for file input and output. A
            file may be missing, inaccessible, malformed, or unexpectedly
            shortened. Chapter 12 will apply these exception-handling patterns
            to those situations.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_recoverable-program-visualization">
        <title>Tracing Recovery at the Boundary</title>

        <p>
            The static listing shows the low-level function reporting the invalid score and the top-level code translating that failure into a useful recovery message.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter11_recovery-exercise-level">
        <statement><p>Where should an exception usually be handled?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Never; all exceptions should be ignored.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Unresolved exceptions can terminate the program.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>At the lowest possible level, even when that level cannot recover.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>The handling level should understand the needed recovery action.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>At a level that knows how to take meaningful action.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A handler should recover, report, retry, or otherwise respond deliberately.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Immediately in every function that might call another function.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Catching without useful action can hide the failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Only inside the compiler.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Exception handling is part of the running program.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_recovery-parsons-boundary">
        <statement><p>Arrange the top-level recovery boundary.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>try {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>process_score(120);</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>}</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>catch (const std::invalid_argument&amp; error) {</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; "Please try again" &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_recovery-exercise-message">
        <statement><p>Which user-facing message is most useful?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Internal compiler object 0xA04F failed.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Implementation details are not useful to most users.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Everything succeeded.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The operation actually failed, so the message would be misleading.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Error.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This does not explain what failed or how to recover.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Please try again: score must be between 0 and 100.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The message explains the problem and suggests the next action.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>The operation failed somewhere.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>A useful message identifies the problem and, when possible, a next step.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_recovery-exercise-guidelines">
        <statement><p>Which practice supports a recoverable program?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Allow invalid objects to circulate until a later function notices.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Classes should protect their invariants early.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Put every recovery decision inside the lowest-level helper.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>Lower-level code should detect problems; higher-level code may know how to recover.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Use exceptions for ordinary loop stopping conditions.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Expected control flow should use ordinary decisions.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Catch every exception and continue as if nothing happened.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Ignoring failure can leave the program's state or output misleading.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Keep low-level detection separate from high-level recovery.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This division lets each layer use the context it has.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
